<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kebra Nagast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kebra Nagast, or Glory of the Kings, is an Ethiopian text compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. It tells of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-of-sheba">Queen of Sheba</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and their son Menelik I, and the bringing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. It provided the Solomonic legitimacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>'s rulers and drew on Aksumite traditions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kebra-nagast.jpg" alt="Kebra Nagast" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_De_ontmoeting_tussen_Solomon_en_Sheba_TMnr_5956-6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian royal tradition of descent from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba, codified in the fourteenth-century <b>Kebra Nagast</b> and traced through the kings of Aksum, shaped Ethiopian identity, statecraft and diplomacy for centuries after the empire's fall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian Empire's singular significance is its demonstration that ideological legitimacy rooted in Solomon, Orthodox Christianity, and the <b>Kebra Nagast</b> can sustain a state through catastrophes that would permanently extinguish any institution lacking that depth of foundational myth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…visiting Solomon, retold in the Qur’an, where she is unnamed but later tradition calls her Bilqis, and in the Ethiopian <b>Kebra Nagast</b>, which makes her the mother of Menelik I, gave Saba a lasting place in world literature and art, although no Sabaean inscription mentions such a queen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Kebra Nagast</b>, “Glory of the Kings”, by the Nebura'ed Yeshaq of Aksum, retold the story of the Queen of Sheba, Solomon and Menelik I; the first contemporary royal chronicles date to Amda Seyon I (1314–1344), and around 200 texts on Ethiopian saints were written between the 14th…</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
